1
00:00:05,490 --> 00:00:10,490
Greetings and welcome to the second week of this MOOC introduction to bio risk management.

2
00:00:10,490 --> 00:00:16,490
In this week I will be discussing the concept of risk assessment.

3
00:00:16,490 --> 00:00:23,490
We will be discussing the various terminologies associated with risk assessment such as accident, incident

4
00:00:23,490 --> 00:00:32,490
as well as specific terminologies such as adequacy and the importance of microbial risk assessment.

5
00:00:32,490 --> 00:00:41,940
Welcome back to the second week of this MOOC introduction to bio risk management.

6
00:00:41,940 --> 00:00:44,940
I hope you have been making good progress.

7
00:00:44,940 --> 00:00:52,940
As this MOOC progresses, we continuously improve the quality of the content based on your comments and suggestions.

8
00:00:52,940 --> 00:00:57,940
I look forward to receiving these comments and suggestions in the forums.

9
00:00:57,940 --> 00:01:02,940
Today's lecture will focus on risk assessment.

10
00:01:03,940 --> 00:01:08,940
I will introduce you to the concepts of hazards and risks.

11
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:16,940
And this will then progress on to the next lecture module, which will focus on risk mitigation.

12
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:24,940
Now, as a bio risk manager, you must be aware of the multiple risks which are present in a laboratory setting.

13
00:01:24,940 --> 00:01:36,940
These extend beyond biological risks and these hazards can compromise your laboratory work as well as your safety.

14
00:01:36,940 --> 00:01:42,940
As a competent bio risk manager, you must assess the risks associated with the hazards,

15
00:01:42,940 --> 00:01:52,740
with the primary objective of minimizing the likelihood of a breach of containment.

16
00:01:52,740 --> 00:01:58,740
In this module, I will introduce you to the concepts of hazards and risks.

17
00:01:58,740 --> 00:02:07,740
I want you to understand how a process involving a biological agent, a laboratory process to be specific,

18
00:02:07,740 --> 00:02:15,120
increases the likelihood and consequence of a particular event.

19
00:02:15,120 --> 00:02:22,120
And that event focuses on the breach of containment and the subsequent exposure of the laboratory personnel

20
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:26,120
and the community to the biological agent.

21
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:30,120
I will introduce you to the process of hazard identification and risk assessment

22
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:36,120
and introduce you to specific terms such as residual risk, accident and incident.

23
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:43,650
Each of these terms has a very specific definition.

24
00:02:43,650 --> 00:02:46,650
These are your learning outcomes.

25
00:02:46,650 --> 00:02:51,650
You should be able to define and describe the terms hazard and risk,

26
00:02:51,650 --> 00:02:54,650
describe how laboratory processes increase the level of risk,

27
00:02:54,650 --> 00:02:58,650
perform a hazard identification and risk assessment,

28
00:02:58,650 --> 00:03:03,650
and define the terms residual risk, accident and incident.

29
00:03:03,650 --> 00:03:11,650
Now, in this lecture module, I have introduced you to the process via a case study.

30
00:03:11,650 --> 00:03:15,650
We will undertake a process, a very simple process,

31
00:03:15,650 --> 00:03:27,240
before we move on to more complex case studies in the next lecture.

32
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:32,240
The first term which you must understand is the term incident.

33
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:40,240
An incident is an event. Both accidents and incidents are events.

34
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:46,240
They occur within a spatial and temporal framework.

35
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:54,240
However, an incident has a potential to cause harm, but it does not necessarily cause harm.

36
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:57,240
This can be in the case of a near miss.

37
00:03:57,240 --> 00:04:02,240
For instance, you are working in the laboratory with a beaker full of sulfuric acid

38
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:09,620
and you spill the acid, but just in time to avoid the spill.

39
00:04:09,620 --> 00:04:15,620
So you spill the acid, but you step back immediately and acid falls on the floor.

40
00:04:15,620 --> 00:04:19,620
Now, this is an example of an incident. It has not caused an accident,

41
00:04:19,620 --> 00:04:28,140
but it has the potential for causing harm in the form of a burn injury.

42
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:34,140
An accident is an unintended event giving rise to harm.

43
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:39,140
Now, for example, you have the same case, you have sulfuric acid on the table,

44
00:04:39,140 --> 00:04:43,140
you spill it and then you have a burn injury on your skin.

45
00:04:43,140 --> 00:04:45,140
That constitutes an accident.

46
00:04:45,140 --> 00:04:49,140
There was no intention to spill the acid on your skin.

47
00:04:49,140 --> 00:04:58,230
However, it occurred, so it is unintended.

48
00:04:58,230 --> 00:05:00,230
We now move on to the definition of hazard.

49
00:05:00,230 --> 00:05:09,230
A hazard is a source situation or act with a potential for causing harm.

50
00:05:09,230 --> 00:05:12,230
A very common example is your vehicle itself.

51
00:05:12,230 --> 00:05:17,230
You are in your vehicle, your car or your truck, in your park, in your garage.

52
00:05:17,230 --> 00:05:20,230
The truck itself is not a hazard,

53
00:05:20,230 --> 00:05:27,230
but when you begin to move this truck across the parking lot into the main street

54
00:05:27,230 --> 00:05:34,230
and onto the highway, the hazard is manifest due to a process.

55
00:05:34,230 --> 00:05:39,540
And these are some of the hazards in the laboratory.

56
00:05:39,540 --> 00:05:44,540
You have theft, you can have fire hazards, radioactive hazard,

57
00:05:44,540 --> 00:05:50,540
ergonomic hazard slipping, as well as the radioactive

58
00:05:50,540 --> 00:05:53,540
and biological hazards and electrical hazards.

59
00:05:53,540 --> 00:05:57,540
These are the hazards which you encounter in a standard laboratory.

60
00:05:57,540 --> 00:06:02,350
Now, we move on to the concept of risk.

61
00:06:02,350 --> 00:06:07,350
Risk is the combination of the probability of occurrence of harm

62
00:06:07,350 --> 00:06:10,350
and the severity of that harm.

63
00:06:10,350 --> 00:06:15,860
I will define and describe this in detail with an example.

64
00:06:15,860 --> 00:06:24,660
A risk focuses on probability and severity.

65
00:06:24,660 --> 00:06:28,910
Probability implies likelihood.

66
00:06:28,910 --> 00:06:30,910
What is the likelihood of the event?

67
00:06:30,910 --> 00:06:36,910
And severity is focused on the possible consequences of that event.

68
00:06:36,910 --> 00:06:40,910
For instance, if you are working with a biological agent,

69
00:06:40,910 --> 00:06:48,910
what is the likelihood of that biological agent causing harm to the laboratory workers

70
00:06:48,910 --> 00:06:50,910
or the community?

71
00:06:50,910 --> 00:06:54,910
Obviously, it will be higher in the case of the laboratory environment

72
00:06:54,910 --> 00:06:57,910
where you work within a confined space.

73
00:06:57,910 --> 00:07:02,910
However, the consequences will be similar to the public

74
00:07:02,910 --> 00:07:05,910
as well as to the laboratory workers.

75
00:07:05,910 --> 00:07:10,910
If the biological agent were to be released into a public space,

76
00:07:10,910 --> 00:07:13,910
it would cause harm to the community,

77
00:07:13,910 --> 00:07:18,910
which is equivalent to the harm that would be caused in a laboratory working environment.

78
00:07:18,910 --> 00:07:22,910
So, the consequences may be similar,

79
00:07:22,910 --> 00:07:26,910
but the likelihood changes based on the process.

80
00:07:26,910 --> 00:07:30,910
Let us delve into this with an example.

81
00:07:30,910 --> 00:07:38,660
By risk is the combination of the probability of the occurrence of the harm

82
00:07:38,660 --> 00:07:40,660
and the severity of that harm

83
00:07:40,660 --> 00:07:44,660
where the source of harm is a biological agent.

84
00:07:44,660 --> 00:07:46,660
Potoxin.

85
00:07:46,660 --> 00:07:51,660
So, this redefines the risk factor in terms of the bio-risk.

86
00:07:51,660 --> 00:07:59,890
Now, hazards evolve into risks.

87
00:07:59,890 --> 00:08:03,890
As I mentioned to you in the example of the vehicle,

88
00:08:03,890 --> 00:08:08,890
your vehicle parked in your parking lot or in your garage does not pose a risk,

89
00:08:08,890 --> 00:08:12,890
but the very fact that you move the vehicle from the parking lot

90
00:08:12,890 --> 00:08:16,890
into an unsafe environment, which is the highway or the freeway,

91
00:08:16,890 --> 00:08:19,890
increases the level of risk.

92
00:08:19,890 --> 00:08:23,890
The hazard evolves into a risk.

93
00:08:23,890 --> 00:08:26,890
Now, in the laboratory setting, laboratory procedures,

94
00:08:26,890 --> 00:08:31,890
shipping of biological agents, theft, and breach of containment,

95
00:08:31,890 --> 00:08:35,890
all are risks which are posed by the hazard

96
00:08:35,890 --> 00:08:42,640
that is essentially the biological agent in itself.

97
00:08:42,640 --> 00:08:46,640
If you have developed this analogy or this example for you,

98
00:08:46,640 --> 00:08:50,640
let us assume that we isolated a microbe from the soil,

99
00:08:50,640 --> 00:08:52,640
but we don't know what identity is.

100
00:08:52,640 --> 00:08:55,640
We culture the microbe in the lab,

101
00:08:55,640 --> 00:08:58,640
and then we move on to the introduction of new genes.

102
00:08:58,640 --> 00:09:01,640
We introduce new genes into this microbe,

103
00:09:01,640 --> 00:09:04,640
and we increase the culture volume in the fermenter.

104
00:09:04,640 --> 00:09:08,640
So, from a small petri plate, which is about 20 cc of media,

105
00:09:08,640 --> 00:09:12,640
we move on to a 25 liter fermenter with liquid culture.

106
00:09:12,640 --> 00:09:16,640
So, now we have up the volume, we increase the volume.

107
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:21,300
Then we inject this microbe into mice

108
00:09:21,300 --> 00:09:24,300
to check whether it's pathogenic or non-pathogenic.

109
00:09:24,300 --> 00:09:27,300
This is a laboratory test or laboratory procedure.

110
00:09:27,300 --> 00:09:30,300
And now there is a potential for transmission from the mice

111
00:09:30,300 --> 00:09:32,300
into the laboratory workers.

112
00:09:32,300 --> 00:09:35,300
So, as you can see, a process,

113
00:09:35,300 --> 00:09:39,300
a simple process of increasing the culture volume

114
00:09:39,300 --> 00:09:43,300
and then infecting another host has increased

115
00:09:43,300 --> 00:09:46,300
the risk of accidental release

116
00:09:46,300 --> 00:09:49,300
of this particular biological agent.

117
00:09:49,300 --> 00:09:52,300
You may also want to look at another risk

118
00:09:52,300 --> 00:09:55,300
from the biosafety and biosecurity perspective,

119
00:09:55,300 --> 00:09:59,300
which is the risk of theft of this particular sample

120
00:09:59,300 --> 00:10:01,300
and the risk of release of the data

121
00:10:01,300 --> 00:10:07,300
pertaining to infection of human hosts via the animal host.

122
00:10:07,300 --> 00:10:13,300
Now, this can pose a potential biosecurity risk

123
00:10:13,300 --> 00:10:16,300
as the information about transmission

124
00:10:16,300 --> 00:10:18,300
from the animal to the human host

125
00:10:18,300 --> 00:10:27,020
can be misused with nefarious or bad intent.

126
00:10:27,020 --> 00:10:30,020
Okay, and with the risk of accidental release,

127
00:10:30,020 --> 00:10:34,020
we have also the risk of the consequences increase

128
00:10:34,020 --> 00:10:41,500
as the experiment progresses.

129
00:10:41,500 --> 00:10:45,500
As a virus manager, we focus on the hazard identification.

130
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:49,500
Then we conduct a risk assessment, risk control,

131
00:10:49,500 --> 00:10:54,500
and we assess the residual risk before we make a decision

132
00:10:54,500 --> 00:10:57,500
whether we want to proceed with that experiment or not.

133
00:10:57,500 --> 00:11:00,500
So, hazard identification involves identification

134
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:03,500
of the hazards in the laboratory.

135
00:11:03,500 --> 00:11:05,500
In our case, as virus managers,

136
00:11:05,500 --> 00:11:08,500
we focus on the potential biological hazards.

137
00:11:08,500 --> 00:11:10,500
We then conduct a risk assessment,

138
00:11:10,500 --> 00:11:13,500
which I will demonstrate to you via case study,

139
00:11:13,500 --> 00:11:15,500
and then we focus on risk control

140
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:18,500
or what is termed as risk mitigation.

141
00:11:18,500 --> 00:11:21,500
This will be the subject of our next lecture

142
00:11:21,500 --> 00:11:24,500
in which we focus on risk control.

143
00:11:24,500 --> 00:11:29,840
And finally, we identify a residual risk

144
00:11:29,840 --> 00:11:31,840
where we determine residual risk

145
00:11:31,840 --> 00:11:34,840
and then make decisions regarding the process.

146
00:11:34,840 --> 00:11:37,840
So, if the residual risk is too high,

147
00:11:37,840 --> 00:11:40,840
a laboratory virus manager may advise the management.

148
00:11:41,840 --> 00:11:44,840
Please do not work with this particular biological agent

149
00:11:44,840 --> 00:11:52,540
because the facilities are not adequate.

150
00:11:52,540 --> 00:11:56,540
Now, the process of recognizing that a hazard exists

151
00:11:56,540 --> 00:11:59,540
and defining its characteristics

152
00:11:59,540 --> 00:12:03,540
is termed as hazard identification.

153
00:12:03,540 --> 00:12:08,540
So, hazard identification basically focuses on identifying

154
00:12:08,540 --> 00:12:10,540
or categorizing a biological agent

155
00:12:10,540 --> 00:12:13,540
into its respective risk group.

156
00:12:13,540 --> 00:12:17,540
And this constitutes the process of defining

157
00:12:17,540 --> 00:12:19,540
its characteristics.

158
00:12:19,540 --> 00:12:23,110
So, in order to identify a hazard,

159
00:12:23,110 --> 00:12:27,110
you need a priori information or prior information

160
00:12:27,110 --> 00:12:31,110
pertaining to that particular biological agent.

161
00:12:31,110 --> 00:12:35,110
And this can be found in the pathogen safety data sheets,

162
00:12:35,110 --> 00:12:38,110
which I will share with you via a link.

163
00:12:38,110 --> 00:12:40,110
So, the pathogen safety data sheets

164
00:12:40,110 --> 00:12:42,110
do not constitute a written document.

165
00:12:42,110 --> 00:12:46,110
It's a website which is being designed and developed

166
00:12:46,110 --> 00:12:48,110
by the government of Canada,

167
00:12:48,110 --> 00:12:51,110
and it's a very useful website for biarisk managers.

168
00:12:51,110 --> 00:12:56,540
We now move on to biarisk management.

169
00:12:56,540 --> 00:12:59,540
So, biarisk management focuses on the process

170
00:12:59,540 --> 00:13:01,540
of evaluating the biarisks

171
00:13:01,540 --> 00:13:04,540
and taking into account the adequacy

172
00:13:04,540 --> 00:13:06,540
of any existing controls

173
00:13:06,540 --> 00:13:10,540
and deciding whether or not the biarisk is acceptable.

174
00:13:10,540 --> 00:13:12,540
So, these are multiple steps.

175
00:13:12,540 --> 00:13:14,540
You first evaluate the biarisk.

176
00:13:14,540 --> 00:13:16,540
You take into account the adequacy

177
00:13:16,540 --> 00:13:18,540
of any existing controls

178
00:13:18,540 --> 00:13:20,540
which may be present at your facility,

179
00:13:20,540 --> 00:13:23,540
and then you decide whether the biarisk

180
00:13:23,540 --> 00:13:25,540
is acceptable or not.

181
00:13:25,540 --> 00:13:33,380
Biarisk controls focuses on actions implementing

182
00:13:33,380 --> 00:13:35,380
biarisk management decisions,

183
00:13:35,380 --> 00:13:39,380
and we will focus on this in a specific lecture

184
00:13:39,380 --> 00:13:41,380
on controls.

185
00:13:41,380 --> 00:13:45,390
These are the five controls.

186
00:13:45,390 --> 00:13:48,390
They are elimination, substitution,

187
00:13:49,390 --> 00:13:52,390
engineering controls, administrative controls,

188
00:13:52,390 --> 00:13:55,390
and personnel protective equipment.

189
00:13:55,390 --> 00:13:58,390
I have just touched upon these briefly

190
00:13:58,390 --> 00:14:00,390
to give you a mental note

191
00:14:00,390 --> 00:14:04,390
of what we will be discussing in the next lecture.

192
00:14:04,390 --> 00:14:07,390
So, all of these controls and their application

193
00:14:07,390 --> 00:14:10,390
will be discussed in the next lecture.

194
00:14:10,390 --> 00:14:17,900
Upon completion of the risk assessment,

195
00:14:17,900 --> 00:14:21,900
we work with what is known as residual risk.

196
00:14:21,900 --> 00:14:26,900
A biarisk manager cannot eliminate risk completely,

197
00:14:26,900 --> 00:14:32,900
as risk involves working with specific biological agents.

198
00:14:32,900 --> 00:14:36,900
So, we have to decide whether we want to proceed

199
00:14:36,900 --> 00:14:37,900
with the experiment

200
00:14:37,900 --> 00:14:39,900
or whether we want to inform the management

201
00:14:39,900 --> 00:14:40,900
that the risk is too high

202
00:14:40,900 --> 00:14:44,900
and terminate the experimental procedure

203
00:14:44,900 --> 00:14:45,900
or the laboratory process.

204
00:14:45,900 --> 00:14:52,170
I will briefly delve into concepts

205
00:14:53,170 --> 00:14:55,170
via a very simple case study

206
00:14:55,170 --> 00:14:58,170
before I move on to more complex case studies

207
00:14:58,170 --> 00:15:00,170
during this MOOC.

208
00:15:00,170 --> 00:15:03,170
But let us look at a very simple case study

209
00:15:03,170 --> 00:15:05,170
just to introduce you to...

210
00:15:05,170 --> 00:15:08,540
Okay, I have set up an example B,

211
00:15:08,540 --> 00:15:11,540
that is all of us are a team of biarisk managers.

212
00:15:11,540 --> 00:15:14,540
So, we have a collective responsibility

213
00:15:14,540 --> 00:15:17,540
and we also have a biosafety officer amongst us.

214
00:15:17,540 --> 00:15:21,540
And our task is to develop a laboratory management system

215
00:15:21,540 --> 00:15:24,540
for working with a known infectious biological agent

216
00:15:24,540 --> 00:15:26,540
for which the following information

217
00:15:26,540 --> 00:15:28,540
is currently available.

218
00:15:28,540 --> 00:15:30,540
Now, I have used the word information

219
00:15:30,540 --> 00:15:33,540
and currently available.

220
00:15:33,540 --> 00:15:36,540
In the case of biological agents

221
00:15:36,540 --> 00:15:38,540
and evolving situations,

222
00:15:38,540 --> 00:15:41,540
you must always refer to the current information

223
00:15:41,540 --> 00:15:45,540
available at the pathogen safety data sheet website

224
00:15:45,540 --> 00:15:49,540
or PSDS website as the information evolves

225
00:15:50,540 --> 00:15:54,540
with increasing amounts of available data.

226
00:15:54,540 --> 00:15:59,270
This is the information which is available to us

227
00:15:59,270 --> 00:16:01,270
in our particular case study.

228
00:16:01,270 --> 00:16:05,270
The microorganism for the causative agent is a virus.

229
00:16:05,270 --> 00:16:07,270
The risk group is risk group four,

230
00:16:07,270 --> 00:16:09,270
refer to WHO risk groups.

231
00:16:09,270 --> 00:16:12,270
The mode of transmission is via aerosol.

232
00:16:12,270 --> 00:16:15,270
The portal of entry is through your nasal passage

233
00:16:15,270 --> 00:16:18,270
as well as through the ocular root through the eyes.

234
00:16:18,270 --> 00:16:20,270
And there is no vaccine available.

235
00:16:20,270 --> 00:16:24,270
So, this is the information which is available to us.

236
00:16:24,270 --> 00:16:26,270
So, based on this information,

237
00:16:26,270 --> 00:16:29,270
we can conduct our risk assessment.

238
00:16:29,270 --> 00:16:33,270
We will delve into a thorough risk assessment

239
00:16:33,270 --> 00:16:35,270
in another case study.

240
00:16:35,270 --> 00:16:46,580
Now, this is the process which occurs at your facility.

241
00:16:46,580 --> 00:16:48,580
So, this is the biological agent.

242
00:16:48,580 --> 00:16:51,580
It is shipped to your facility.

243
00:16:51,580 --> 00:16:54,580
So, we have a transport security here.

244
00:16:54,580 --> 00:16:57,580
Then it is dispensed into tubes.

245
00:16:57,580 --> 00:16:59,580
So, we open up the packaging

246
00:16:59,580 --> 00:17:01,580
and we dispense it into tubes

247
00:17:01,580 --> 00:17:03,580
and then we store it in a refrigerator.

248
00:17:03,580 --> 00:17:05,580
So, I have indicated this as a safe

249
00:17:05,580 --> 00:17:07,580
because this is secure refrigerator.

250
00:17:07,580 --> 00:17:09,580
Then it moves on to process.

251
00:17:09,580 --> 00:17:13,580
So, the laboratory technicians or the researchers

252
00:17:13,580 --> 00:17:15,580
begin processing the sample over here.

253
00:17:15,580 --> 00:17:19,580
And finally, they transfer the data into the cloud

254
00:17:19,580 --> 00:17:23,580
via a secure computer or secure internet connection.

255
00:17:23,580 --> 00:17:26,580
And this process generates waste.

256
00:17:26,580 --> 00:17:33,380
So, this is the process which you must define as a BiRisk Manager.

257
00:17:33,380 --> 00:17:38,380
You must break down a process into its constituent parts

258
00:17:38,380 --> 00:17:43,380
and approach each process separately during risk assessment.

259
00:17:43,380 --> 00:17:51,130
This is a verbose definition of the processes.

260
00:17:51,130 --> 00:17:53,130
I have delineated the processes.

261
00:17:53,130 --> 00:17:55,130
So, the biological agent is shipped.

262
00:17:55,130 --> 00:17:57,130
The sample is received.

263
00:17:57,130 --> 00:17:59,130
The sample is stored.

264
00:17:59,130 --> 00:18:02,130
Extraction is done and processing of the RNA.

265
00:18:02,130 --> 00:18:04,130
And finally, the sample is disposed

266
00:18:04,130 --> 00:18:07,130
and concurrently documentation and transfer to the hospital.

267
00:18:07,130 --> 00:18:10,130
The data is transferred back to the hospital.

268
00:18:10,130 --> 00:18:19,810
The first step as a BiRisk Manager is to look at adequacy.

269
00:18:19,810 --> 00:18:22,810
Now, suppose I receive that sample in a lab

270
00:18:22,810 --> 00:18:25,810
in which I do not have what I mentioned earlier,

271
00:18:25,810 --> 00:18:28,810
a biological safety cabinet.

272
00:18:28,810 --> 00:18:30,810
I basically cannot work with that sample

273
00:18:30,810 --> 00:18:33,810
because when I open the sample and I process it,

274
00:18:33,810 --> 00:18:36,810
there will be the generation of aerosols.

275
00:18:36,810 --> 00:18:43,410
So, in this case, the case of adequacy must be addressed.

276
00:18:45,490 --> 00:18:48,490
We now move on to other aspects.

277
00:18:48,490 --> 00:18:49,490
So, we look at facilities.

278
00:18:49,490 --> 00:18:51,490
Are our facilities adequate?

279
00:18:51,490 --> 00:18:54,490
Do we have the appropriate instrumentation?

280
00:18:54,490 --> 00:18:56,490
Are our personnel competent?

281
00:18:56,490 --> 00:18:58,490
Do they need additional training?

282
00:18:58,490 --> 00:19:01,490
Do we have the SOPs and the means for disposal

283
00:19:01,490 --> 00:19:04,490
of biological waste or hazardous waste?

284
00:19:04,490 --> 00:19:07,490
And do we have contingency planning

285
00:19:07,490 --> 00:19:10,490
in the event of a breach of containment?

286
00:19:10,490 --> 00:19:16,490
These are factors which must be looked into in detail

287
00:19:16,490 --> 00:19:18,490
when you conduct your risk assessment.

288
00:19:18,490 --> 00:19:21,490
So, we look at our existing controls

289
00:19:21,490 --> 00:19:26,740
as the first step of risk assessment.

290
00:19:26,740 --> 00:19:31,590
We then move on to specific questions.

291
00:19:31,590 --> 00:19:35,590
So, risks we are attempting to assess.

292
00:19:35,590 --> 00:19:39,590
So, the first risk may be biosafety associated.

293
00:19:39,590 --> 00:19:42,590
So, risk of exposure of the laboratory personnel

294
00:19:42,590 --> 00:19:43,590
to the biological agent.

295
00:19:43,590 --> 00:19:46,590
So, this concerns personnel.

296
00:19:46,590 --> 00:19:51,260
Second risk, risk of exposure of the community

297
00:19:51,260 --> 00:19:53,260
to the biological agent.

298
00:19:53,260 --> 00:19:57,260
As I mentioned earlier, you may recall the concept

299
00:19:57,260 --> 00:19:59,260
of primary and secondary containment.

300
00:20:00,260 --> 00:20:05,260
So, in the first case is our primary containment adequate.

301
00:20:05,260 --> 00:20:09,260
In the second case is our secondary containment adequate.

302
00:20:09,260 --> 00:20:12,260
We may also want to look at the biosecurity risk,

303
00:20:12,260 --> 00:20:16,260
risk of intentional release of the biological agent.

304
00:20:16,260 --> 00:20:20,260
So, someone may decide to steal that biological asset

305
00:20:20,260 --> 00:20:26,260
and use it for their own intended purpose

306
00:20:26,260 --> 00:20:28,260
which may be nefarious.

307
00:20:29,260 --> 00:20:31,260
With the intent to cause harm.

308
00:20:31,260 --> 00:20:36,720
So, let's look back at our example or case study

309
00:20:36,720 --> 00:20:41,720
and risk assessment is a step by step process.

310
00:20:41,720 --> 00:20:44,720
We conduct risk assessment at each step.

311
00:20:44,720 --> 00:20:48,720
So, the first step is here transport, storage

312
00:20:48,720 --> 00:20:55,600
and then we receipt of the sample and storage

313
00:20:55,600 --> 00:20:58,600
and processing of the sample and waste management.

314
00:20:58,600 --> 00:21:03,910
So, the risk of release of the biological agent

315
00:21:03,910 --> 00:21:06,910
and risk of exposure of the personnel

316
00:21:06,910 --> 00:21:10,910
as well as the community to the biological agent

317
00:21:10,910 --> 00:21:15,910
must be addressed at each stage or each step of the process.

318
00:21:15,910 --> 00:21:19,930
This is a risk matrix.

319
00:21:19,930 --> 00:21:21,930
I have developed this risk matrix for you

320
00:21:21,930 --> 00:21:24,930
to be used throughout the MOC.

321
00:21:24,930 --> 00:21:27,930
You may refer to other risk matrix

322
00:21:27,930 --> 00:21:29,930
which are available online.

323
00:21:29,930 --> 00:21:33,930
But we look at risk of exposure of laboratory personnel

324
00:21:33,930 --> 00:21:37,930
to the biological agent during processing of the sample.

325
00:21:37,930 --> 00:21:40,930
You must define all these parameters.

326
00:21:40,930 --> 00:21:44,930
The risk of exposure of the laboratory personnel

327
00:21:44,930 --> 00:21:48,930
to the biological agent during.

328
00:21:48,930 --> 00:21:54,930
And we look at the consequence and the likelihood.

329
00:21:54,930 --> 00:21:59,930
So, if the consequence is low, we indicate a low risk.

330
00:21:59,930 --> 00:22:02,930
If it is very high, you have a scale

331
00:22:02,930 --> 00:22:05,930
and the likelihood is on this scale.

332
00:22:05,930 --> 00:22:08,930
So, this is low, moderate, high, very high.

333
00:22:08,930 --> 00:22:12,930
So, we have clearly indicated scale.

334
00:22:12,930 --> 00:22:15,930
For instance, the risk of exposure of laboratory personnel

335
00:22:15,930 --> 00:22:20,930
to the biological agent during, for instance, extraction of RNA.

336
00:22:20,930 --> 00:22:22,930
The risk of exposure will be very high

337
00:22:22,930 --> 00:22:25,930
and the consequences will be moderate

338
00:22:25,930 --> 00:22:28,930
because RNA is not infectious.

339
00:22:29,930 --> 00:22:33,930
However, if the same laboratory worker was involved

340
00:22:33,930 --> 00:22:39,930
in culture of the live virus in a liquid medium,

341
00:22:39,930 --> 00:22:42,930
the consequence will be very high

342
00:22:42,930 --> 00:22:47,930
and the likelihood of that sample getting released

343
00:22:47,930 --> 00:22:51,930
or spilled accidentally also is very high.

344
00:22:51,930 --> 00:22:54,930
So, this is a risk matrix which we will delve into

345
00:22:54,930 --> 00:22:57,930
during the course of this case study.

346
00:22:57,930 --> 00:23:03,010
We also have to look at the overall risk matrix.

347
00:23:03,010 --> 00:23:06,010
Now, in the overall risk matrix, we look at the process

348
00:23:06,010 --> 00:23:08,010
which are shipping, receipt of the samples,

349
00:23:08,010 --> 00:23:11,010
storage, processing, disposal and data transfer

350
00:23:11,010 --> 00:23:13,010
and we look at the overall risk.

351
00:23:13,010 --> 00:23:16,010
So, this indicates low, high and moderate.

352
00:23:16,010 --> 00:23:19,010
I have indicated this in various colors

353
00:23:19,010 --> 00:23:21,010
for your reference.

354
00:23:21,010 --> 00:23:23,010
And for the overall risk matrix,

355
00:23:23,010 --> 00:23:27,010
we look at the risk of exposure of the community

356
00:23:27,010 --> 00:23:33,010
to the biological agent and the risk of the theft

357
00:23:33,010 --> 00:23:36,010
of the sample or the intentional release

358
00:23:36,010 --> 00:23:39,010
in terms of the biosecurity aspect.

359
00:23:39,010 --> 00:23:42,010
So, there's a high risk during shipping

360
00:23:42,010 --> 00:23:45,010
and a high risk during receipt and storage.

361
00:23:45,010 --> 00:23:48,010
It drops and during processing of the sample,

362
00:23:48,010 --> 00:23:51,010
disposal and data transfer, the risk is very low.

363
00:23:51,010 --> 00:23:56,010
So, the risk level drops as the sample is processed.

364
00:23:57,010 --> 00:24:00,010
As the sample gets inactivated,

365
00:24:00,010 --> 00:24:04,010
the thief can no longer use it

366
00:24:04,010 --> 00:24:08,010
for intended harm or intentional release

367
00:24:08,010 --> 00:24:11,010
as it is no longer biologically active.

368
00:24:11,010 --> 00:24:16,500
At this point, we have to make a decision.

369
00:24:16,500 --> 00:24:20,500
And the questions which a virus manager looks at are,

370
00:24:20,500 --> 00:24:22,500
what is the residual risk?

371
00:24:22,500 --> 00:24:24,500
Are our facilities adequate?

372
00:24:24,500 --> 00:24:26,500
Do we need additional training?

373
00:24:26,500 --> 00:24:29,500
And we then move on to the next step,

374
00:24:29,500 --> 00:24:31,500
which is risk mitigation.

375
00:24:31,500 --> 00:24:38,430
So, for risk mitigation, as I mentioned earlier,

376
00:24:38,430 --> 00:24:41,430
we'll focus on the mitigation of risk

377
00:24:41,430 --> 00:24:44,430
by the application of five controls.

378
00:24:44,430 --> 00:24:50,340
To summarize, in this lecture,

379
00:24:50,340 --> 00:24:52,340
we have focused on the hazard

380
00:24:52,340 --> 00:24:56,230
and not only biological hazards,

381
00:24:56,230 --> 00:24:58,230
but also other laboratory hazards,

382
00:24:58,230 --> 00:25:01,230
such as electrical, chemical,

383
00:25:01,230 --> 00:25:04,230
radiological and economic hazards.

384
00:25:04,230 --> 00:25:06,230
We have focused on the concept of risk

385
00:25:06,230 --> 00:25:09,230
and defined risk in terms of risk itself

386
00:25:09,230 --> 00:25:11,230
as well as bio-risk.

387
00:25:11,230 --> 00:25:14,230
We have conducted a basic risk assessment

388
00:25:14,230 --> 00:25:18,230
using a risk matrix in a step-by-step manner.

389
00:25:18,230 --> 00:25:22,230
We have understood the concept of residual risk

390
00:25:22,230 --> 00:25:26,230
and we have reinforced all these concepts

391
00:25:26,230 --> 00:25:29,860
via a case study.

392
00:25:29,860 --> 00:25:32,860
We have also learned about the risk matrix

393
00:25:32,860 --> 00:25:34,860
and how it can be utilized

394
00:25:34,860 --> 00:25:38,860
to identify risks associated

395
00:25:38,860 --> 00:25:42,860
with specific biological or laboratory processes.

396
00:25:42,860 --> 00:25:46,860
That brings us to the end of this lecture.

397
00:25:46,860 --> 00:25:50,860
I hope you have understood the key concepts.

398
00:25:50,860 --> 00:25:52,860
If you need more clarification

399
00:25:52,860 --> 00:25:54,860
pertaining to any of these concepts,

400
00:25:54,860 --> 00:25:57,860
please post a remark in the forum

401
00:25:57,860 --> 00:25:59,860
or comment in the forum.

402
00:25:59,860 --> 00:26:02,860
I welcome your critical comments and suggestions

403
00:26:02,860 --> 00:26:05,860
and I will constantly try to improve upon

404
00:26:05,860 --> 00:26:08,860
this MOC as it progresses.

405
00:26:08,860 --> 00:26:15,380
Thank you for your support and your feedback.

406
00:26:15,380 --> 00:26:17,380
Thank you and stay bio-safe.
